# Line endings.
#
# Several test suites read a source file and assert against its text with a
# bare \n. With core.autocrlf on — which is the Windows default and is set on
# this machine — those files arrive in the working tree as CRLF while the
# repository stores LF, so the assertions failed locally and passed in CI. That
# is the worst way round: the developer sees red for something that is not
# wrong, and stops trusting the suite.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` settles it. Git still stores LF as it always did; the
# difference is that it now checks LF out too, whatever autocrlf says locally,
# so every machine sees the same bytes the tests were written against.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The two that genuinely need CRLF. A .bat or .cmd with LF endings can fail in
# ways that are hard to read — cmd.exe mis-parses a trailing label or a
# multi-line block — so these keep the platform's own.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binaries, marked so Git never inspects or converts them. Without this a file
# that happens to look textual can be "normalised" and corrupted, and the
# damage only shows when something tries to open it.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary
*.mov binary
*.mp3 binary
*.wav binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.eot binary
*.pdf binary
*.onnx binary

# Shell scripts were already pinned before this file grew; kept explicit
# because it is the one case where the wrong ending is not a cosmetic problem
# but a script that will not run at all.
*.sh text eol=lf
